# Copy to .env and fill in. Never commit the real file - .env is gitignored.
# Loaded automatically by src/cli.py on every run (python-dotenv) - no need
# to export these into your shell yourself.
#
# Full step-by-step for both credentials below: docs/SETUP.md
#
# TradersPost owns the credential that can actually place an order (its own
# Coinbase connection, configured inside TradersPost, never in this repo).
# This repo only ever holds a read-only key - see BROKER_API_KEY_CRYPTO below.

# Webhook URL for the crypto universe's paper strategy in TradersPost.
# config/universe.yaml -> webhook_url_env names which variable a given
# universe reads; a second asset class adds a second variable here.
TRADERSPOST_WEBHOOK_URL_CRYPTO=

# Reconciliation only (cli.py reconcile) - see SPEC.md "Secrets" for why
# this exists at all. This key MUST be scoped read-only / view-only: no
# trade permission, no transfer/withdrawal permission. src/broker/client.py
# refuses to use a key that isn't.
#
# Coinbase's current API keys (Coinbase Developer Platform) are not a
# simple key+secret pair - the JSON you download has a "name" field and a
# "privateKey" field. Map them like this:
#   BROKER_API_KEY_CRYPTO    = the "name" field, e.g. organizations/xxxx/apiKeys/xxxx
#   BROKER_API_SECRET_CRYPTO = the "privateKey" field, the whole PEM block
# The private key is multi-line - wrap it in double quotes so it survives
# as one value, exactly like the example (blank) below shows the shape of:
# BROKER_API_SECRET_CRYPTO="-----BEGIN EC PRIVATE KEY-----
# ...
# -----END EC PRIVATE KEY-----"
BROKER_API_KEY_CRYPTO=
BROKER_API_SECRET_CRYPTO=
